answer.en:
#### Next.js

In Next.js, dynamic routes are created using square brackets (\[\]) in the app/ folder structure. This lets you build pages with URL parameters without manually configuring routes.

1. Basic dynamic route

```txt
app/posts/[id]/page.tsx → /posts/123
```

```tsx
export default function Page({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  return <div>Post ID: {params.id}</div>;
}
```

- id — the route parameter
- the value is available via params

1. Multiple parameters

```txt
app/users/[userId]/posts/[postId]/page.tsx
→ /users/42/posts/7
```

```tsx
export default function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: { userId: string; postId: string };
}) {
  return (
    <div>
      User: {params.userId}, Post: {params.postId}
    </div>
  );
}
```

1. Catch-all routes

Used for a variable number of segments.

```txt
app/docs/[...slug]/page.tsx
→ /docs/a/b/c
```

```tsx
export default function Page({ params }: { params: { slug: string[] } }) {
  return <div>{params.slug.join('/')}</div>;
}
```

1. Optional catch-all routes

```txt
app/docs/[[...slug]]/page.tsx
```

Works for:

- /docs
- /docs/a
- /docs/a/b

1. Data fetching in dynamic routes

```tsx
export default async function Page({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const post = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/posts/${params.id}`, {
    cache: 'no-store',
  }).then(res => res.json());

  return <h1>{post.title}</h1>;
}
```

1. Generating static routes (SSG)

For SSG, you use generateStaticParams:

```typescript
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  return [{ id: '1' }, { id: '2' }];
}
```

#### Best practices

- Use Server Components by default
- Validate route parameters
- Don’t overuse catch-all routes
- Combine with layouts for shared UI

In short:

- Dynamic routes are created via \[param\]
- params contains URL values
- Catch-all and optional routes are supported
- Works with SSR, SSG, and ISR

