answer.en:
#### Next.js

In Next.js, API routes are implemented via Route Handlers (app/api/\*/route.ts) and are based on the standard Web Fetch API. TypeScript is used to type:

- request body
- response
- route params (params)
- query parameters

1. Basic API route with TypeScript

```typescript
// app/api/posts/route.ts
export async function GET(): Promise<Response> {
  return Response.json({ posts: [] });
}
```

Next.js can infer types automatically, but explicitly returning Promise<Response> is a good practice.

1. Typing the request body (POST)

```typescript
type CreatePostBody = {
  title: string;
  content: string;
};

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const body: CreatePostBody = await request.json();

  return Response.json({
    success: true,
    title: body.title,
  });
}
```

Important: request.json() returns any, so you need to specify the type manually.

1. Typing route params (params)

For the route:

```bash
app/api/posts/[id]/route.ts
```

```typescript
type RouteParams = {
  params: {
    id: string;
  };
};

export async function GET(
  request: Request,
  { params }: RouteParams
) {
  return Response.json({ id: params.id });
}
```

1. Typing query parameters

```typescript
export async function GET(request: Request) {
  const { searchParams } = new URL(request.url);
  const page = Number(searchParams.get('page') ?? 1);

  return Response.json({ page });
}
```

For more complex cases, you can define a type:

```typescript
type Query = {
  page?: string;
  limit?: string;
};
```

1. Typing the response

```typescript
type PostResponse = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
};

export async function GET(): Promise<Response> {
  const data: PostResponse = {
    id: '1',
    title: 'Hello',
  };

  return Response.json(data);
}
```

1. Using shared type utilities

It is recommended to extract types into shared files:

```txt
types/
 └─ api.ts
```

```typescript
export type CreateUserBody = {
  email: string;
  password: string;
};
```

1. What is not used in Next.js 16+

- NextApiRequest
- NextApiResponse
- pages/api
- Express-like types

The App Router uses standard:

- Request
- Response

#### Best practices

- Always type request.json()
- Extract types into types/
- Type params for dynamic routes
- Avoid legacy Pages Router types

In short:

- API routes in the App Router use standard Request and Response
- TypeScript types body, params, and responses
- Legacy Pages Router types are not used
- Types are best kept in separate files

