}
```

1. Використання утиліт для типів

Рекомендовано виносити типи:

```txt
types/
 └─ api.ts
```

```typescript
export type CreateUserBody = {
  email: string;
  password: string;
};
```

1. Що не використовується в Next.js 16+

- NextApiRequest
- NextApiResponse
- pages/api
- Express-подібні типи

App Router використовує стандартні:

- Request
- Response

#### Best practices

- Завжди типізувати request.json()
- Виносити типи у types/
- Типізувати params для динамічних маршрутів
- Не використовувати legacy типи з Pages Router

Коротко:

- API-роути в App Router використовують стандартні Request і Response
- TypeScript типізує body, params і відповіді
- Legacy типи Pages Router не використовуються
- Типи краще виносити в окремі файли

answer.en:
#### Next.js

In Next.js, type safety is achieved by combining:

- a strict TypeScript configuration
- typing both server and client logic
- shared types for API and data
- type checking during build

The goal is a type-safe data flow from DB → server → UI.

1. Enable strict mode

In tsconfig.json:

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true
  }
}
```

This is the foundation of type safety.

1. Type pages and routes

```tsx
type PageProps = {
  params: { id: string };
  searchParams: { page?: string };
};

export default function Page({ params }: PageProps) {
  return <div>{params.id}</div>;
}
```

1. Type your data fetching

```tsx
type Post = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
};

async function getPost(id: string): Promise<Post> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/posts/${id}`);
  return res.json();
}
```

Never leave any in place.

1. Share types between client and server

```txt
types/
 └─ post.ts
```

```typescript
export type Post = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
};
```

Use them:

- in Route Handlers
- in Server Components
- in Client Components

1. Type API routes

```typescript
type CreatePostBody = {
  title: string;
};

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const body: CreatePostBody = await request.json();
  return Response.json(body);
}
```

1. Runtime validation (important)

TypeScript checks at compile time, but not at runtime. For full safety, use schemas (for example, Zod).

```tsx
import { z } from 'zod';

const schema = z.object({
  title: z.string(),
});

const body = schema.parse(await request.json());
```

1. Type Server Actions

```typescript
'use server';

type FormDataType = {
  email: string;
};

export async function submit(data: FormDataType) {
  // type-safe
}
```

1. Avoid any

If you need a temporary escape hatch:

```typescript
unknown // better than any
```

1. Type env variables

Create:

```typescript
env.d.ts
declare namespace NodeJS {
  interface ProcessEnv {
    DATABASE_URL: string;
  }
}
```

1. Build-time checks

Next.js runs type checking during:

```bush
npm run build
```

If there are errors, the build fails.

#### Architectural principles for type safety

- Server Components as the source of truth
- shared types (types/)
- runtime validation for external data
- minimal Client Components
- no any

In short:

- Enable strict mode
- Use shared types for server/client
- Type API, pages, and data fetching
- Add runtime validation for external data
- Do not use any

